WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
Uber

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:04.770
Uber’s meteoric rise to dominate the personal
transport market has made it one of the most

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.500
successful - and the most hated - companies
in history.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.130
Along with its controversial CEO Travis Kalanick,
it has developed a reputation as being iron-fisted,

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:17.460
cruel and relentless in its pursuit of power.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.590
Inspiring an equal measure of admiration and
loathing, Uber has turned the traditional

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.039
taxi industry on its head - and provided consumers
with a more user friendly way of getting from

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:33.470
Point A to point B.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:38.629
Genesis

00:00:38.629 --> 00:00:43.609
In the mid-1990’s, with the world Wide Web
still in its infancy, the undergraduate Computer

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:50.109
Science Association or USCA at UCLA University
in sunny California was a breeding ground

00:00:50.109 --> 00:00:51.460
for future millionaires.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.109
The association was more of a clubhouse where
like-minded students could relax after class

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:58.579
while they played games and talked computers.

00:00:58.579 --> 00:01:03.579
Though not a founding member, a young San
Fernando Valley resident by the name of Travis

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:06.850
Kalanick soon become a central figure of this
group.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:10.740
Kalanick was pursuing a double major in computers
and business.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.700
Despite the boom in Internet companies up
north in Silicon Valley, the USCA was mostly

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.770
about fun and games.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.310
Soon, however its members stumbled upon an
opportunity.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.450
Because there were no firewalls back then,
it was possible to search for files on all

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:26.460
connected computers and then index the results.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.640
The group decided to replicate the time honoured
practice of college kids lending out their

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.810
record albums.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:37.009
But they were able to extend the lending fraternity
to anybody who was connected to the internet.

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:40.600
The idea was molded into a start up which
they named Scour.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:45.189
It was a tool that would scrub a network’s
files looking for MP3s.

00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:49.380
Travis Kalanick was not involved in the founding
of Scour because he was doing an internship

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:50.380
with Intel.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:54.439
But as Scour became established and grew in
popularity, he jumped on board.

00:01:54.439 --> 00:01:56.649
He was in charge of marketing and fund-raising.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:00.649
It was in this role that Kalanick developed
the on the job training that would serve him

00:02:00.649 --> 00:02:01.999
in later years.

00:02:01.999 --> 00:02:06.240
Living at his parents’ house he would spend
up to 8 hours a day on the phone, wheeling

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.640
and dealing to attract investors.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.440
In 1999, his efforts paid off when Hollywood
power player Michael Ovitz poured $4 million

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.880
into Scour in return for a 51% share in the
company.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.780
Within a year, however, the writing was on
the wall for Scour.

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:26.850
It’s success had attracted a competitor
who would do things better - Napster.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.330
Scour's fundamental problem was that it didn’t
scale.

00:02:29.330 --> 00:02:35.130
The site would often crash through overuse,
and Scour was running out of fresh content.

00:02:35.130 --> 00:02:38.370
Meanwhile Napster was paying close attention
to Scour’s problems.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.130
They introduced a tweak whereby listening
to a song would automatically make it available

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:43.130
to others.

00:02:43.130 --> 00:02:46.630
As a result Napster quickly became a viral
sensation.

00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:51.350
Scour tried to gain ground by moving into
sharing movie files, but ultimately they were

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:52.350
doomed.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:55.690
They company went into bankruptcy in 2000.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:58.450
Red Swoosh

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.560
By the end of that year, with Scour gone,
twenty-four year old Travis Kalanick, had

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.960
no college degree and no job.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.500
He needed a new plan - and he needed it fast.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.410
Within weeks of the folding of Scour, Kalanick
was onto his next project.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:16.730
He teamed with former Scour partner Michael
Todd to form Red Swoosh, which was essentially

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:19.510
Scour recreated for business customers.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.030
This new startup would allow companies to
use the Internet to share similarly large

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.900
files with their customers.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:30.630
Enabling large file movement was already big
business, but Red Swoosh’s competitive advantage

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:36.480
was that it capitalized on the asset light
strategy to keep its costs below that of competitors.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.630
Red Swoosh pushed and pulled files from personal
computers owned by others, foreshadowing what

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:46.710
Uber would later do in connecting riders with
drivers whose cars represented ‘massive

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.210
unused capital’ on city streets.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.660
As CEO of Red Swoosh, Kalanick’s job was
to pitch to potential clients.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.530
As he had done at Scour, he set a frenetic
work pace, proving to be the quintessential

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:58.530
workaholic.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:03.340
Despite his gargantuan efforts, however, Red
Swoosh, became a victim of the peaking of

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.740
the Internet bubble.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.050
Investments dried up and tensions rose.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:12.090
Kalanick blamed Todd for mismanagement and
wrangled for him to be kicked out of the company.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.870
Kalanick kept wheeling and it looked as if
he had found a saviour.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:19.680
On September 11th, 2001 he was scheduled to
meet with a potential partner investor named

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.720
Danny Lewin.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:25.700
Lewin, however, was on board American Airlines
Flight 11 from Boston to Los Angeles, which

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:28.340
crashed into the north tower of the World
Trade Center.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:31.950
It is thought that he was the first to die,
after confronting the hijackers.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.900
By the end of 2001, Red Swoosh was out of
money.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:39.170
Reprieve came in the form of a father and
son team who had made a fortune from a semiconductor

00:04:39.170 --> 00:04:41.620
business - James and Jared Chao.

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:46.040
They agreed to pour $300,000 into the business
in return for a 30% stake.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:50.850
Over the next few years, Red Swoosh stumbled
along, propped up by further investment.

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:56.330
Then, in April 2007, major competitor Akamai
purchased the company, hiring the handful

00:04:56.330 --> 00:04:59.270
of employees of the company, including Kalanick.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:00.810
Kalanick was now 31 years old.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:05.510
For his share in the sale of Red Swoosh he
had pocketed around $3 million.

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:11.320
Yet, he was now tied to a large corporation
as an employee - something he had never experienced

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:12.320
before.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.900
Over the next year he worked to continue building
the Red Swoosh brand.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:19.889
At the same time he re-styled himself as an
‘angel investor’ for young entrepreneurs.

00:05:19.889 --> 00:05:24.670
He bought an old house in the Castro, an old
San Francisco neighborhood and named it the

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:25.670
‘Jam Pad.’

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:29.810
The house became a meeting place for this
with fresh tech based ideas and no money.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:34.490
Kalanick revelled in the role of wiser, older
sage who had been there and done that.

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:37.770
His blog, which he called Swooshing, became
essential reading.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:42.200
In it he described his home, the Jam Pad as
‘ a place where entrepreneurs regularly

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:46.750
come to hang out, to rap on ideas, to jam
with other entrepreneurs, to play Wii Tennis

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:52.200
and Gears of War and to have fantastic healthy
gourmet meals made by the Jam Pad’s in-house

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.200
chef.’

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.150
Kalanick quit Red Swoosh in late 2008.

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:59.889
He spent the next couple of years biding his
time as he examined new business ventures.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:01.730
He also threw himself into leisure activities.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.620
He became a part of a group called the Random
Travellers Society.

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:09.020
They would randomly spin a globe to decide
where in the world they would travel.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:13.410
In 2010, Kalanick got heavily involved with
a Q and A website called Formspring.

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:16.310
He invested in the company, but was soon running
the show.

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:19.650
At the same time, he was still looking out
for other opportunities.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:24.440
He was introduced to a smartphone app started
by an old friend named Garrett Camp.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.540
The app was called UberCab and it allowed
the user to make quick contact with a limousine

00:06:28.540 --> 00:06:30.010
in the San Francisco area.

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:33.840
Kalanick began sharing his time between Formspring
and UberCab.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:36.490
Within a few months it became obvious that
he had to choose between them.

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:40.700
The people at Formspring were pushing him
to become their CEO, but in late September,

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:45.840
2010 he walked into a board meeting and declared,
“I’m going to be Uber.”

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.230
Uber Begins

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:53.960
In the Summer of 2008, Garrett Camp, a mild
mannered Canadian software designer was waiting

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.260
for a cab in San Francisco’s bay area.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:07:00.020
He’d been waiting nearly thirty minutes
for a cab that had promised to be there in

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.220
fifteen.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:05.699
When he saw an empty cab from a competitor
company come by he hailed it and got in.

00:07:05.699 --> 00:07:08.970
A few minutes later, though, he got a call
from the original taxi company asking where

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:09.970
he was.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:12.810
He replied that they were late so he’d gotten
in another cab.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.060
This became a regular occurrence for Camp.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:19.280
Eventually both taxi companies that he dealt
with, Luxor and Yellow, blacklisted him.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:23.790
This annoyed the young Canadian, who just
happened to have $75 million in his bank account

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.720
after selling his first start-up, StumbleUpon.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.730
Hailing a cab was now more difficult for Camp.

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:32.230
The taxi switchboard companies would cut him
off as soon as they saw his number.

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:36.520
One evening, while standing on a street corner
and running late for a date, he had the proverbial

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:37.520
light bulb moment.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.990
He had his brand new Apple iPhone in his hand.

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.550
That iPhone was GPS enabled.

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.700
Why couldn’t he request a cab directly from
his phone, simply by pushing a button that

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.170
says ‘Pick Me Up’?

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.650
This way you cut out the dispatcher completely.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:54.639
Camp also knew that the iPhone included a
thing called an accelerometer.

00:07:54.639 --> 00:07:59.060
This would allow for the tracking of not just
where a car was but how fast it was moving.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:03.960
With this technology, a map could be generated
that showed the beginning and end of the trip,

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:05.380
along with its duration and price.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:10.250
It would also link the rider with the driver
- cutting out the middleman.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:15.500
On August 8th, 2008 Camp paid $35 to reserve
the website, www.ubercab.com.

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:20.040
He then began to elicit feedback about his
idea from his circle of entrepreneurial friends.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.100
He also interviewed taxi drivers, who complained
about the hours they were sitting idly waiting

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:25.100
for a fare.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:27.190
Slowly other people began jumping on board.

00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.080
One of them was Travis Kalanick.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:34.779
In early 2009, Travis contributed an idea
that became pivotal to the future Uber model.

00:08:34.779 --> 00:08:38.990
Rather than buying a fleet of vehicles, which
Camp was envisioning, Kalanick told him that

00:08:38.990 --> 00:08:44.300
he didn’t need to buy cars - just give the
app to drivers and let then be free agents.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.249
They would work when they wanted to work.

00:08:46.249 --> 00:08:50.920
The focus for both men was on limousine rides,
rather than normal taxi cab rides.

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.639
Kalanick signed on as an advisor to Uber,
with a 10% stake in the company.

00:08:54.639 --> 00:08:56.720
It took over a year to develop the app.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.450
By January, 2010 Camp and Kalanick began testing
the service.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:04.600
They also began making plans to launch operations
in San Francisco.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:09.619
Kalanick hired a 27-year-old General Electric
middle management employee named Ryan Graves

00:09:09.619 --> 00:09:12.080
to oversee the San Francisco launch.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:15.319
Graves began visiting garages to sign up limo
drivers.

00:09:15.319 --> 00:09:20.350
The service launched in late May, 2010 with
just a handful of drivers and customers.

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:24.019
The initial customer base came from Twitter
followers who had been keeping tabs on what

00:09:24.019 --> 00:09:25.519
the entrepreneurs were up to.

00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:30.100
Kalanick and Graves who, had been made CEO,
began working together to find investors.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:34.529
After receiving a lot of knockbacks, the seed
funding round raised $1.25 million.

00:09:34.529 --> 00:09:38.720
It was at this point that Kalanick decided
to go full time with UberCab.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:43.040
But he wasn't content to play second fiddle
to a guy that he had hired.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:47.910
He orchestrated a demotion for Graves and
claimed the title of CEO for himself.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:52.360
He also demanded that his shareholding rise
from 10% to 23%.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:56.431
On the very day that Kalanick became CEO,
the company received a cease and desist letter

00:09:56.431 --> 00:10:01.129
from the San Francisco Metro Transit Authority
and California Public Utilities Commission.

00:10:01.129 --> 00:10:05.160
It stated that the name ‘UberCab’ meant
that they were a taxicab company and under

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.889
the authority’s direction.

00:10:06.889 --> 00:10:09.220
Kalanick solved the problem in 5 seconds.

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:13.319
He changed the name from UberCab to simply
Uber.

00:10:13.319 --> 00:10:18.680
At the time of his taking over, the operation
was tiny - and it made plenty of mistakes.

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:22.019
On Halloween of 2010, they offered a discount
to riders.

00:10:22.019 --> 00:10:26.480
This created such a huge demand, on their
already busiest day yet, that it left a lot

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.220
of people unable to get a ride.

00:10:28.220 --> 00:10:31.199
Despite this, growth was steady throughout
2010.

00:10:31.199 --> 00:10:35.939
Then, over the 2011 New Year period it absolutely
blew up.

00:10:35.939 --> 00:10:40.139
Word of mouth was responsible for the exponential
growth in Uber’s customer base.

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:42.939
But finding Limo drivers was a different story.

00:10:42.939 --> 00:10:46.939
They didn’t have the same social media habits
as the young about towner’s in need of a

00:10:46.939 --> 00:10:47.939
limo ride.

00:10:47.939 --> 00:10:50.980
Only licensed drivers were eligible to drive
for Uber.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.959
They all had customers of their own and none
of them were prepared to go exclusive with

00:10:54.959 --> 00:10:55.959
Uber.

00:10:55.959 --> 00:10:59.410
Uber began giving away iPhones preloaded with
the Uber app to drivers.

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:03.720
This enabled them to increase their driver
supply to keep up with the ever growing demand

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:05.089
in San Francisco.

00:11:05.089 --> 00:11:09.560
Having proven the model in Frisco, Kalanick
started to replicate it in other cities.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:14.100
Under the guidance of head of operations,
Ryan Graves, they turned their attention on

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:17.500
Seattle, Chicago, Boston, and Washington,
D.C.

00:11:17.500 --> 00:11:20.839
In each city, Graves made connections in the
tech world fist.

00:11:20.839 --> 00:11:23.570
This created buzz and attracted cutting edge
users.

00:11:23.570 --> 00:11:28.029
Once the word was being generated, they began
looking for drivers and working through government

00:11:28.029 --> 00:11:29.199
regulations.

00:11:29.199 --> 00:11:33.699
As they moved from city to city, the Uber
team began refining their playbook, refining

00:11:33.699 --> 00:11:36.050
the system and making it more efficient.

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:42.249
The process became (1) establish buzz in the
tech community (2) assess regulations (3)

00:11:42.249 --> 00:11:48.910
recruit drivers (4) develop pricing (5) woo
local media, and (6) capitalize with social

00:11:48.910 --> 00:11:49.930
media buzz.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:54.119
In the early days, Kalanick was a master at
local public relations.

00:11:54.119 --> 00:11:57.749
Promotion would start with a blog post, in
which he would relate how much he loved the

00:11:57.749 --> 00:11:59.589
city they were moving into.

00:11:59.589 --> 00:12:03.290
He would highlight its key attributes and
then talked about how much greater the city

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:06.360
would be when Uber had introduced their service.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.959
By 2011, the job of securing investment was
much easier.

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:13.360
In February Benchmark Capital poured $10 million
into the company.

00:12:13.360 --> 00:12:17.059
At this point Uber was valued at $60 million.

00:12:17.059 --> 00:12:22.350
The company had nine thousand customers and
$1.8 million in net revenue.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:27.779
It was estimated that it would get to $100
million gross revenue within a year.

00:12:27.779 --> 00:12:31.040
It achieved that goal in six months.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.399
Enemies Emerge

00:12:34.399 --> 00:12:38.809
As Uber sprang up in more and more cities
across America, it began to receive push-back.

00:12:38.809 --> 00:12:41.700
The sixth city that they went into was Washington,
D.C.

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:45.060
Before they could get off the ground, however,
the city’s taxi commissioner announced that

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:47.879
Uber was operating an illegal taxi operation.

00:12:47.879 --> 00:12:52.819
On Martin Luther King Day, 2011 the commissioner
ordered an Uber which took him to a press

00:12:52.819 --> 00:12:55.100
conference at the Mayflower Hotel.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:59.420
He then proceeded to impound the driver’s
car and issued a $20,000 ticket.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:02.130
But Uber didn’t back down.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.829
They assured all drivers that they would pay
all of their fines and took the moral high

00:13:05.829 --> 00:13:11.170
ground in public, asserting that they were
not a taxi company and, therefore, not subject

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.170
to regulatory provisions.

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:18.800
In one city after another, taxi companies,
the taxi commission and local and state agencies

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:20.699
came at Uber to shut it down.

00:13:20.699 --> 00:13:24.189
Under the guidance of consultant Bradley Tusk,
Uber fought back.

00:13:24.189 --> 00:13:28.499
They ran social media campaigns to stop the
opposers, often meeting them in court.

00:13:28.499 --> 00:13:31.740
The public were overwhelmingly on the side
of the company.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:36.050
But there was one thing that Uber’s customers
were not happy about.

00:13:36.050 --> 00:13:40.470
During busy periods, such as rush hour and
bar closing times, it would apply ‘surge

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:43.930
pricing’ with rates going up during these
peak demand times.

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:47.779
Surge pricing had the effect of encouraging
more drivers to work and encouraging some

00:13:47.779 --> 00:13:49.990
customers to find a cheaper service.

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:52.790
Both of these results eased demand on the
service.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:55.100
But it also infuriated the customer base.

00:13:55.100 --> 00:14:00.239
Many of them blamed the unfairness of surge
pricing at the foot of CEO Kalanick, who quickly

00:14:00.239 --> 00:14:05.160
became known as a cutthroat businessman who
refused to listen to consumer sentiment.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:09.269
By the end of 2012, Uber was operating in
27 locations.

00:14:09.269 --> 00:14:13.660
In that year they also introduced UberX, providing
rides in hybrid cars.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:17.339
But it was also the year that Uber faced its
first serious competition.

00:14:17.339 --> 00:14:20.560
It came in San Francisco from a young company
called Lyft.

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:25.299
Lyft was a ride sharing business whereby a
person would slap a large pink moustache on

00:14:25.299 --> 00:14:29.339
the grill of their car and then pick up those
who were in need of a ride for the shared

00:14:29.339 --> 00:14:32.240
cost of gas and a little extra donation.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.829
Usually this would work out to be less than
a cab fare for the same distance.

00:14:35.829 --> 00:14:38.860
It used a smartphone app that was modelled
off of the Uber app.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:43.240
Lyft was a hit with young people, who preferrred
its friendlier, more laid back image.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:45.670
Riding in a limo was starting to look elitist.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:48.839
As a result, Uber set about restructuring
its game plan.

00:14:48.839 --> 00:14:54.199
It would rebrand itself with a focus on getting
a rider from Point A to Point as quickly and

00:14:54.199 --> 00:14:55.910
as inexpensively as possible.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:15:00.970
UberX, which had started out as an eco-friendly
alternative to a limo, became the means of

00:15:00.970 --> 00:15:03.279
beating traditional taxi services.

00:15:03.279 --> 00:15:08.209
In early 2014, Kalanick hired former Amazon
executive Jeff Holden as product development

00:15:08.209 --> 00:15:09.209
manager.

00:15:09.209 --> 00:15:13.410
Holdren identified two things he found lacking
in the Uber system; the ability of the rider

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:18.089
to communicate beforehand where he wanted
to go and the driver’s ability to navigate

00:15:18.089 --> 00:15:19.089
there.

00:15:19.089 --> 00:15:24.709
He oversaw two new features to fix this - destination
input by the rider and turn-by-turn navigation

00:15:24.709 --> 00:15:26.079
on the driver app.

00:15:26.079 --> 00:15:33.369
Both features were a hit and by the end of
2014 Uber was operating in 262 markets worldwide.

00:15:33.369 --> 00:15:35.769
Uber was by now a genuine phenomenon.

00:15:35.769 --> 00:15:39.589
But it was a phenomenon that had attracted
a lot of enemies.

00:15:39.589 --> 00:15:44.619
A lot of that animosity was thanks to the
brash nature of CEO Travis Kalanick.

00:15:44.619 --> 00:15:49.339
His outspokenness and inability to hold his
tongue soon made him the offender in chief.

00:15:49.339 --> 00:15:53.799
Kalanick was the poster boy for political
incorrectness, once stating that he could

00:15:53.799 --> 00:15:57.209
land women as quickly as a person could summon
an Uber.

00:15:57.209 --> 00:16:02.059
Kalanick’s divisive personality ony underscored
Uber’s growing reputation as a bully in

00:16:02.059 --> 00:16:03.129
the marketplace.

00:16:03.129 --> 00:16:07.790
Reports were publicized of some underhanded
tactics used to undermine competitor Lyft.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:12.089
Publications began labelling Uber as an evil
company and describing its CEO as an ‘Ayn

00:16:12.089 --> 00:16:13.879
Rand loving libertarian nut job.’

00:16:13.879 --> 00:16:17.959
In some cities it become politically correct
to avoid Uber all together.

00:16:17.959 --> 00:16:20.120
Uber was in a strange position.

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:22.929
Worldwide its business was growing from strength
to strength.

00:16:22.929 --> 00:16:26.489
At the same time, however, its reputation
was tanking.

00:16:26.489 --> 00:16:31.489
Things went from bad to abysmal when an Uber
driver in Delhi was convicted of raping a

00:16:31.489 --> 00:16:32.489
passenger.

00:16:32.489 --> 00:16:33.809
Something had to be done - quick.

00:16:33.809 --> 00:16:39.870
In early 2015, Uber brought in a new executive
to oversee digital and physical safety, Joe

00:16:39.870 --> 00:16:40.870
Sullivan.

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:44.559
He had a long to-do list, including stricter
management of driver activity.

00:16:44.559 --> 00:16:48.850
Things were tightened up to the extent that
Uber management can tell if a driver is holding

00:16:48.850 --> 00:16:53.050
his phone while driving or braking too hard
or going too fast.

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:57.739
During that year another new executive, Sally
Kay set about taking and, and overcoming,

00:16:57.739 --> 00:17:02.810
a slew of local regulators who were intent
on stopping Uber in its tracks.

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:06.740
By the beginning of 2016, Uber was on a more
even keel.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.199
Rather than lurching from one crisis to another.

00:17:09.199 --> 00:17:14.480
In an attempt to change its image, the company
teamed with Mothers Against Drunk Driving

00:17:14.480 --> 00:17:18.449
to encourage young people to take an Uber
rather than drink and drive.

00:17:18.449 --> 00:17:22.350
It also partnered with hospitals to make it
cheaper for cancer patients to return home

00:17:22.350 --> 00:17:23.959
from treatment.

00:17:23.959 --> 00:17:27.659
The Driverless Future

00:17:27.659 --> 00:17:32.960
Travis Kalanick has been working on introducing
driverless cars to the Uber mix since 2013.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:37.220
He loved the idea that it would no longer
rely on that most uncontrollable of business

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:39.090
variables - people.

00:17:39.090 --> 00:17:44.299
Autonomous vehicles would be far safer as
robots are not subject to drowsiness or distraction.

00:17:44.299 --> 00:17:46.039
And robots would be cheaper than people.

00:17:46.039 --> 00:17:49.340
But in 2013, the technology wasn’t yet ready.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:54.020
By February 2014, Uber was canvassing the
world looking for top robotics talent.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:59.730
It purchased a Pittsburg company called Carnegie
Robotics, making it the kernel of its self-driving

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:00.730
car team.

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:07.220
Then, in December 2014, Uber hired 60 researchers
from the National Robotics Engineering center.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:12.610
After two years of development, Uber announced
in August 2016, that its cars were ready for

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:13.769
a limited showing.

00:18:13.769 --> 00:18:18.210
The cars worked but still required a human
engineer behind a steering wheel should something

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:19.210
go wrong.

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:21.420
Uber are still working on their driverless
car.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:26.440
In that quest they are in a race with the
likes of Google, Apple and Tesla, which is

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:29.260
rumoured to be considering its own ride sharing
business.

00:18:29.260 --> 00:18:33.290
All of this competition will, ultimately,
result in a better, cheaper service for those

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:56.630
of us wanting to get from Point A to Point
B as efficiently 

00:18:56.630 --> 00:19:12.299
and cost effectively as possible.

